ff artifact.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8c884a9d4b094b8e\",\"cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee\",\"cmd-80300483b53d475f\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Login sessions for appworker are limited to 8192 open files and 256 processes (soft and hard)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Playbook wrote /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf with soft/hard nofile 8192 and soft/hard nproc 256 (cmd-8c884a9d4b094b8e, confirmed present in cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee). A real SSH login session as appworker (not just su, which failed for lacking a terminal) shows ulimit -Hn/-Sn=8192/8192 and -Hu/-Su=256/256 (cmd-9782e0fe807d43d8), directly demonstrating the limits are enforced for login sessions via pam_limits (login/sshd PAM stacks confirmed to include pam_limits.so in cmd-1664633a489f49a3).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8c884a9d4b094b8e\",\"cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee\",\"cmd-9782e0fe807d43d8\",\"cmd-1664633a489f49a3\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Implementation performed via Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An Ansible inventory/playbook (/root/ansible-appworker) was executed from node2 (control node with ansible-core 2.20) against node1. Because node1 runs Python 3.5, which is incompatible with ansible-core 2.20's module execution (demonstrated by repeated module deserialization failures in cmd-2861f13cf2674b9b and cmd-63f3b8c2f31d4927), the executor rewrote the playbook using ansible.builtin.raw tasks (cmd-3c0a316b3a51455a), which is a legitimate SSH-only Ansible mechanism. The playbook is idempotent (second run changed=0, cmd-80300483b53d475f), consistent with standard Ansible task semantics.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8f251b92c51244a2\",\"cmd-2861f13cf2674b9b\",\"cmd-63f3b8c2f31d4927\",\"cmd-3c0a316b3a51455a\",\"cmd-8c884a9d4b094b8e\",\"cmd-80300483b53d475f\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"(Independent protocol) Node-restart evidence: pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered state for all managed nodes\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For node1: pre-restart baseline captured boot_id 8336c3a1... with user/limits present (cmd-c6f97d3c7d794e8d), shutdown -r now acknowledged (cmd-33c0e5f7dbd44dc5), disruption confirmed via connection refused (cmd-94d1c54830fe4e4c), new boot_id 463c248a... observed on reconnect (cmd-df28cd2192034b7e), and recovered state confirmed both via file/user checks (cmd-3844138cade3412e) and a fresh live SSH login session re-showing 8192/8192/256/256 (cmd-bda73f4bb13d4d0f). For node2: pre-restart baseline boot_id 416c5cf5... with functional ansible-core (cmd-1621404e9c3a42ea), reboot acknowledged (cmd-06dd77a73e084475), disruption via connection timeout (cmd-b5afb92d58184099), new boot_id 14e14fd7... (cmd-3fcb84dfe6e34a29), and recovered control-node tooling shown by a successful idempotent playbook re-run against node1 (cmd-d5bbae027eda4e8d) plus node1 state re-check (cmd-4fb2be58965f4c4a). These boot-id transitions match the fixed global before/after-executor snapshots exactly, cross-confirming the restart evidence.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c6f97d3c7d794e8d\",\"cmd-33c0e5f7dbd44dc5\",\"cmd-94d1c54830fe4e4c\",\"cmd-df28cd2192034b7e\",\"cmd-3844138cade3412e\",\"cmd-bda73f4bb13d4d0f\",\"cmd-1621404e9c3a42ea\",\"cmd-06dd77a73e084475\",\"cmd-b5afb92d58184099\",\"cmd-3fcb84dfe6e34a29\",\"cmd-d5bbae027eda4e8d\",\"cmd-4fb2be58965f4c4a\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map clearly to task prerequisites (SSH key wiring for control-node access), the core implementation (user creation, limits file), or bounded evidence collection that was promptly reverted (temporary SSH keys placed under /home/appworker/.ssh for live login-session verification in cmd-07101a0891774c34 and cmd-f857e08731634d3f were both removed immediately after in cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee and cmd-eedb82b5815b419f). One minor unnecessary mutation was left unreverted: cmd-63f3b8c2f31d4927 sed-edited inventory.ini to add ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python2.7 while troubleshooting Python-module incompatibility; this approach was abandoned in favor of raw tasks (cmd-3c0a316b3a51455a), but the stale interpreter line was never removed from inventory.ini. This is low-risk residue confined to a scratch control-node inventory file with no security or functional impact, but it was not cleaned up. Several other commands (cmd-2861f13cf2674b9b, cmd-9963d5bbb6464be6, cmd-f64154eea7214f77, cmd-8961aa9072ca47cc, cmd-f4b9c30fe905479e, cmd-8b73c5185f6549b4, cmd-225033a086944274, cmd-6310496098c049d0) were read-only diagnostics or failed attempts that made no persistent state change.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-63f3b8c2f31d4927\",\"cmd-3c0a316b3a51455a\",\"cmd-07101a0891774c34\",\"cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee\",\"cmd-f857e08731634d3f\",\"cmd-eedb82b5815b419f\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The task's material outcomes — creating the appworker account on node1, enforcing 8192 open-file and 256-process login-session limits, and implementing this via Ansible — are all directly and coherently evidenced, including a real SSH login session proving enforcement rather than relying solely on configuration files. The executor also completed the independent node-restart evidence protocol for both node1 and node2 with baseline, disruption, changed boot identity, and recovered state, cross-confirmed by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The only ding is a small unrevoked troubleshooting mutation (a stale ansible_python_interpreter line added to inventory.ini during Python-compatibility debugging) that was superseded but not cleaned up.\",\"limitations\":[\"The executor's use of ansible.builtin.raw tasks instead of higher-level modules (user, pam_limits) was necessitated by node1's Python 3.5 being incompatible with the control node's ansible-core 2.20; this is a reasonable and evidenced workaround but represents a deviation from the most idiomatic Ansible module usage.\",\"Package-health and listening-socket global snapshots show no anomalies, but these are supplemental lifecycle checks rather than task-specific verification.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Create the appworker account on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"ansible-playbook run (cmd-8c884a9d4b094b8e) created the user via a raw useradd task (changed=2); follow-up id appworker (cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee) confirms uid=1001(appworker) exists. Re-run (cmd-80300483b53d475f) shows idempotent skip, confirming the account is the stable managed state, not a one-off artifact.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8c884a9d4b094b8e","cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee","cmd-80300483b53d475f"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Login sessions for appworker are limited to 8192 open files and 256 processes (soft and hard)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Playbook wrote /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf with soft/hard nofile 8192 and soft/hard nproc 256 (cmd-8c884a9d4b094b8e, confirmed present in cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee). A real SSH login session as appworker (not just su, which failed for lacking a terminal) shows ulimit -Hn/-Sn=8192/8192 and -Hu/-Su=256/256 (cmd-9782e0fe807d43d8), directly demonstrating the limits are enforced for login sessions via pam_limits (login/sshd PAM stacks confirmed to include pam_limits.so in cmd-1664633a489f49a3).","evidence_ids":["cmd-8c884a9d4b094b8e","cmd-17cd38e05ff345ee","cmd-9782e0fe807d43d8","cmd-1664633a489f49a3"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Implementation performed via Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"An Ansible inventory/playbook (/root/ansible-appworker) was executed from node2 (control node with ansible-core 2.20) against node1. Because node1 runs Python 3.5, which is incompatible with ansible-core 2.20's module execution (demonstrated by repeated module deserialization failures in cmd-2861f13cf2674b9b and cmd-63f3b8c2f31d4